{"timestamp": "2026-08-28T05:38:54.003+00:00", "event": "llm.request", "session_id": "sess_2c022427-3876-4f70-9bc8-6ac0bd8ab7b8", "user_id": "USR_999", "model_id": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "raw_prompt": "\n        TASK-LEVEL SUFFICIENCY AND EVIDENCE SELECTION\n        You are a groundedness auditor for a read-only Horizon Bank FAQ assistant.\n        Evaluate EACH supplied task independently for whether its supplied\n        parents are response-ready: they must contain enough materially\n        relevant, grounded information for the assistant to give the customer\n        a useful FAQ response.\n\n        Original User Message (advisory context only; never use it as an\n        individual task's completeness target): i just want to know can i apply again if got rejected\n        Trusted Conversation Context: Last Session Recall (advisory only; not current state):\nNone\n\nCurrent-Session Rolling Context (advisory only):\nNone\n\nCurrent Session Trusted History (latest 10 turns):\nUser: I am asking about re-applying for a credit card.\nAI: I do not currently have the details available regarding the process for re-applying for a credit card. 💳\nUser: i just want to know can i apply again if got rejected\n        Task Inputs: [{\"task_id\": \"task_1\", \"task\": {\"task_id\": \"task_1\", \"procedural\": \"Submit a new credit card application through the online portal after the cooling-off period expires.\", \"policy\": \"Applicants must meet current income requirements and pass internal credit assessment criteria for new approvals.\", \"conceptual\": \"Credit card eligibility is determined by individual financial history and internal risk assessment policies at application.\", \"target_commercial_product\": \"Credit_Card\", \"target_intent\": [\"Eligibility\", \"Application\"], \"customer_component\": \"Inquire about the eligibility to re-apply for a credit card after a previous rejection.\", \"direct_query\": \"Inquire about the eligibility to re-apply for a credit card after a previous rejection.\", \"subquery_id\": \"subquery_1\", \"angle_groups\": [{\"group_id\": \"base\", \"procedural\": \"Submit a new credit card application through the online portal after the cooling-off period expires.\", \"policy\": \"Applicants must meet current income requirements and pass internal credit assessment criteria for new approvals.\", \"conceptual\": \"Credit card eligibility is determined by individual financial history and internal risk assessment policies at application.\"}]}, \"retained_partial_parent_ids\": [], \"prior_sufficiency_audit_history\": [], \"parents\": [{\"parent_id\": \"faq_1b9ab045\", \"content\": \"Question: Why did Horizon Bank reject my credit card application?\\n\\nAnswer: - Every credit card request undergoes an internal review, and we evaluate each submission thoroughly. Typical reasons for a denial include:\\n- Insufficient earnings:\\n  • Singapore citizen or Permanent Resident aged {{faq_1b9ab045_001}}‑{{faq_1b9ab045_002}} with yearly income under {{faq_1b9ab045_003}}\\n  • Singapore citizen or Permanent Resident aged {{faq_1b9ab045_004}}+ with yearly income under {{faq_1b9ab045_005}}\\n  • Non‑resident ({{faq_1b9ab045_006}} years or older) earning less than {{faq_1b9ab045_007}} annually\\n- Having already reached the maximum credit exposure allowed across all banks\\n- Existing overdue obligations with other financial institutions\\n- Absence of a recent Notice of Assessment or CPF contribution record\\n- Applicant is younger than {{faq_1b9ab045_008}} years old\\n\\nIf you were declined, you may still consider opening a Horizon savings account, which offers up to {{faq_1b9ab045_009}} p.a. on the first {{faq_1b9ab045_010}} deposited and can provide up to an {{faq_1b9ab045_011}} return when paired with a Horizon debit card (subject to terms and conditions). To start the savings account application, simply:\\n- Uninstall the Horizon App\\n- Re‑install the Horizon App from the app store\\n- Submit a savings‑account request through the app\\n\\nPlease note that Singapore‑dollar deposits held by non‑bank depositors are protected by the Singapore Deposit Insurance Corporation up to {{faq_1b9ab045_012}} per depositor across all Scheme members, as mandated by law.\"}, {\"parent_id\": \"faq_dcf718fa\", \"content\": \"Question: Am I qualified to apply for a Horizon Bank credit card?\\n\\nAnswer: - Applicants aged {{faq_dcf718fa_001}} to {{faq_dcf718fa_002}} may submit a credit‑card request if they meet one of the following income criteria:\\n  • Singapore citizen or permanent resident: minimum annual earnings of {{faq_dcf718fa_003}}.\\n  • Foreign national holding a valid work pass: minimum annual earnings of {{faq_dcf718fa_004}}.\\n- Applicants older than {{faq_dcf718fa_005}} are also eligible provided they satisfy one of these conditions:\\n  • Singapore citizen or permanent resident: minimum annual earnings of {{faq_dcf718fa_006}}.\\n  • Foreign national holding a valid work pass: minimum annual earnings of {{faq_dcf718fa_007}}.\"}, {\"parent_id\": \"faq_b3c9ded6\", \"content\": \"Question: What are the requirements for applying for a Horizon Bank credit card?\\n\\nAnswer: To be considered for a Horizon Bank credit card, you must meet specific criteria. If you are a Singaporean citizen or permanent resident between the ages of {{faq_b3c9ded6_001}} and {{faq_b3c9ded6_002}}, you will need to have an annual income of at least {{faq_b3c9ded6_003}}. For foreigners with a valid work pass, the requirement is an annual income of {{faq_b3c9ded6_004}}. Alternatively, if you are above {{faq_b3c9ded6_005}} years old, the annual income requirements are {{faq_b3c9ded6_006}} for Singaporean citizens or permanent residents, and {{faq_b3c9ded6_007}} for foreigners with a valid work pass.\"}, {\"parent_id\": \"faq_93d37190\", \"content\": \"Question: After opening a savings account, am I eligible to apply for a Horizon credit card?\\n\\nAnswer: - Eligibility criteria:\\n  - You are between {{faq_93d37190_001}} and {{faq_93d37190_002}} years old and earn at least {{faq_93d37190_003}} annually, and you are a Singapore citizen or permanent resident.\\n  - You are older than {{faq_93d37190_004}} years and earn at least {{faq_93d37190_005}} annually, and you are a Singapore citizen or permanent resident.\\nOnly Singapore citizens and permanent residents can hold both a Horizon savings account and a Horizon credit card. To submit an application, go to **More > More for you** within the Horizon App.\"}]}]\n\n        Rules:\n        - Each Task Input contains `task.customer_component`: the intent\n          formulator's resolved sub-query for that task. Evaluate a task ONLY\n          against its own `customer_component` and that task's candidate\n          parents.\n        - Each Task Input may include `prior_sufficiency_audit_history` from\n          earlier attempts for the same task in this turn. Read it before\n          judging the current candidates and remain consistent with its prior\n          sufficiency judgments. This history is internal audit context only;\n          it is not customer-facing evidence and does not authorize any new\n          factual claim.\n        - Classify each task as SUFFICIENT, PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT, or\n          INSUFFICIENT. Judge whether the supplied parents provide useful,\n          materially relevant information for the task—not whether they repeat\n          the customer's wording or explicitly resolve every possible\n          interpretation of the customer's question.\n        - PARTIAL-EVIDENCE DECISION ORDER: First identify whether any supplied\n          parent contains a factual proposition directly relevant to a\n          meaningful aspect of the customer's resolved request. If one does,\n          do not return INSUFFICIENT merely because the parent cannot determine\n          the customer's requested outcome, permission, eligibility,\n          exception, legality, preferred action, or other broader conclusion.\n          Retain that parent as PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT when its supported fact\n          can be truthfully presented as useful, bounded Horizon guidance\n          while another material aspect remains unresolved. Continue retrieval\n          for the unresolved aspect.\n        - Return INSUFFICIENT only when no supplied parent contains any\n          factual proposition that can be truthfully and materially presented\n          in relation to the resolved request. A parent is not material merely\n          because it concerns the same product: its supported fact must\n          directly concern the same topic, condition, process, boundary, or\n          factual premise in the customer's request.\n        - The reverse matters just as much: a parent whose own subject is a\n          distinctly-named, different product or service is not material\n          merely because it is credit/loan-adjacent, belongs to the same\n          broader family, or is reached through the same in-app menu path as\n          the customer's named product. For example, a balance transfer\n          draws on loan credit and is filed under the app's \"Loan\" section\n          alongside a personal loan application -- but a parent describing\n          how to request a balance transfer does not materially answer a\n          request about applying for a (personal/general) loan. Do not let\n          shared category vocabulary or menu wording (\"Loan section\",\n          \"commence your application\") substitute for the parent actually\n          being about the specific product or process the customer named.\n          Only treat a differently-named product's parent as material when\n          it explicitly states it also applies to, or is the general form\n          of, the customer's named product.\n        - SUFFICIENT: the selected parents contain enough grounded information\n          for a useful response to the `customer_component`, even when that\n          response is an explicit policy boundary, limit, condition,\n          restriction, or other related factual information rather than a\n          literal yes/no answer. The response generator will state only what\n          the evidence supports and must not invent a broader conclusion.\n        \nPRECISION REFERENCE CONTRACT:\n- A token matching `{{faq_*}}` is a database-backed exact-value reference,\n  never missing, unknown, incomplete, or a customer-visible template.\n- It may represent an exact amount, rate, limit, date, threshold, or other\n  precise value. Treat it as the underlying approved fact for relevance,\n  sufficiency, generation, compliance, and groundedness.\n- Preserve the token exactly in any draft. The backend deterministically\n  substitutes its authoritative value only after outbound validation.\n- Never reject, weaken, or describe evidence as unavailable merely because the\n  exact value is represented by a `{{faq_*}}` token.\n- Within the stated scope, \"up to {{faq_*}}\" expresses an exact maximum. It\n  supports the bounded conclusion that the stated coverage or benefit does not\n  exceed that value. It does not, by itself, support claims about applications,\n  approvals, denials, exceptions, or who controls the limit.\n\n        - PARTIALLY_SUFFICIENT: selected parents provide grounded, material\n          information for one meaningful part of the `customer_component`,\n          while another material part has no useful related evidence. Select\n          only the supporting parent IDs; the backend will retain them while\n          retrieval continues.\n        - INSUFFICIENT: the supplied parents provide no grounded, materially\n          relevant information that could be usefully presented in response to\n          the `customer_component`.\n        - Use the ordinary meaning of explicit policy boundaries, quantities,\n          conditions, and restrictions when deciding whether a task is\n          response-ready. Do not require a parent to restate the cu